WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.455
A direct-mapped cache controller stores recently used memory blocks to reduce
repeated access latency.

00:00:07.455 --> 00:00:13.313
This prototype uses one kilobyte of cache data and sixteen-byte blocks.

00:00:13.313 --> 00:00:21.834
The actual group members are Shaurya Sharma, roll number 2401010439, and Lakshay
Saharan, roll number 2401020034.

00:00:21.834 --> 00:00:24.496
Shaurya handles implementation and analysis.

00:00:24.496 --> 00:00:28.224
Lakshay handles the report, slides and video.

00:00:28.224 --> 00:00:33.549
This walkthrough uses synthetic narration and actual recorded simulation
evidence..

00:00:34.149 --> 00:00:38.521
The cache sits between a CPU request interface and backing memory.

00:00:38.521 --> 00:00:43.290
The address decoder separates a twenty-two-bit tag, six-bit index and four-bit
offset.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.674
Metadata stores tags and valid bits.

00:00:45.674 --> 00:00:48.058
The comparator checks for a hit.

00:00:48.058 --> 00:00:50.840
Data RAM holds four words per line.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:54.019
Requests and responses use valid and ready handshakes.

00:00:54.019 --> 00:00:56.801
The controller supports one outstanding CPU request..

00:00:57.401 --> 00:00:59.696
This is the actual Verilog implementation.

00:00:59.696 --> 00:01:04.669
The top module instantiates the decoder, metadata storage, tag comparator and
data RAM.

00:01:04.669 --> 00:01:11.172
The data RAM has synchronous read and write ports, so the FPGA flow can infer
block memories.

00:01:11.172 --> 00:01:17.293
A word is read when the CPU request is accepted, then LOOKUP uses that
registered word.

00:01:17.293 --> 00:01:20.735
The dedicated test memory is excluded from cache synthesis..

00:01:21.335 --> 00:01:24.387
IDLE accepts a request and LOOKUP checks the line.

00:01:24.387 --> 00:01:26.421
A read hit prepares a response.

00:01:26.421 --> 00:01:33.542
A read miss alternates REFILL REQUEST and REFILL WAIT until all four words
arrive, then installs the tag and valid bit.

00:01:33.542 --> 00:01:35.237
Writes use write-through with no-write-allocate.

00:01:35.237 --> 00:01:39.645
A write hit updates cache and memory; a write miss updates memory only.

00:01:39.645 --> 00:01:42.357
The response waits until the CPU accepts it..

00:01:42.957 --> 00:01:46.369
These PASS lines come from the executed testbench.

00:01:46.369 --> 00:01:49.354
Eight workloads run on two cache configurations.

00:01:49.354 --> 00:01:53.618
Independent Verilog and Python scoreboards check hit decisions and data.

00:01:53.618 --> 00:01:58.309
The testbench also checks backing memory and stable outputs during stalls.

00:01:58.309 --> 00:02:01.294
Reset tests cover invalidation and aborted transactions.

00:02:01.294 --> 00:02:08.544
Including the no-cache baseline and FPGA primitive netlist, eighteen thousand
five hundred and four trace transactions passed..

00:02:09.144 --> 00:02:13.091
This waveform comes directly from the simulator V C D file.

00:02:13.091 --> 00:02:18.473
The first read misses and causes four memory responses before the CPU response
becomes valid.

00:02:18.473 --> 00:02:22.061
Later accesses to other words in the same block hit.

00:02:22.061 --> 00:02:25.290
CPU response backpressure keeps the result stable until ready.

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:32.467
A separate checker samples every directed-trace response handshake and confirms
that all eighteen match the checked result C S V..

00:02:33.067 --> 00:02:35.851
Here are actual requests from the directed trace.

00:02:35.851 --> 00:02:38.287
The first read at address zero misses.

00:02:38.287 --> 00:02:42.812
Reads at four, eight and twelve hit because the whole block was refilled.

00:02:42.812 --> 00:02:48.381
Address hexadecimal four hundred maps to the same line with a different tag,
causing a conflict.

00:02:48.381 --> 00:02:51.513
Later writes and reads verify write-through and no-write-allocate behavior.

00:02:51.513 --> 00:02:54.994
Every returned word and hit flag matches the independent model..

00:02:55.594 --> 00:02:57.933
Sequential reads achieve seventy-five percent hits.

00:02:57.933 --> 00:03:03.000
Repeated reads over a small working set achieve ninety-six point eight eight
percent.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.339
Alternating conflicting addresses achieve zero percent.

00:03:05.339 --> 00:03:15.084
The no-cache comparison is important: repeated reads are about one point eight
three times faster, but sequential and conflict accesses are slower with this
cache.

00:03:15.084 --> 00:03:20.152
A miss fetches four serial words while an uncached read fetches only one..

00:03:20.752 --> 00:03:27.795
The reference implementation targets a Lattice ice forty H X eight K, C T two
fifty-six device.

00:03:27.795 --> 00:03:32.766
It uses six hundred and ten logic cells and four block memories.

00:03:32.766 --> 00:03:38.980
The post-route internal maximum frequency estimate is 75.35 megahertz, meeting
the requested twenty-five megahertz target.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:42.709
IO pins are placed automatically, without external delay constraints.

00:03:42.709 --> 00:03:46.851
These are tool estimates, not measurements from a physical board..

00:03:47.451 --> 00:03:55.310
The repository contains the modular source code, testbenches, traces, waveform,
raw results, IEEE-format report and this demonstration video.

00:03:55.310 --> 00:04:00.549
Run python three scripts slash run dot py to reproduce functional verification.

00:04:00.549 --> 00:04:07.534
Run scripts slash run underscore F P G A dot py for reference synthesis and
timing.

00:04:07.534 --> 00:04:13.646
The actual group has two members, so the five-member rubric requirement needs
faculty confirmation.

00:04:13.646 --> 00:04:17.139
A live presentation remains the group members responsibility..
